{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>For as when a man rises against his fellow.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory explained to us the reason for this lengthiness by way of the parable of the murderer. For it would have sufficed to give the reason regarding her violation, since it says 'the betrothed girl cried out'; therefore they expounded it as 'this comes to teach and is found to be learned' — that is, the murderer comes to teach about the betrothed girl, and is in turn found to learn from her: they draw an analogy of the betrothed girl to the murderer. Just as the murderer — let him be killed and not transgress — that is, if a coercer commands a person to kill his fellow or be killed himself, he is obligated to surrender himself to death and not kill, because it is a matter of reason (sekhel), as Rava said: 'What do you see — that your blood is redder? Perhaps the blood of that other man is redder.' Therefore one does not push aside one life for the sake of another life. So too the betrothed girl: if a person tells her to submit to him or be killed, let her be killed and not transgress. And even regarding a married woman, our Rabbis of blessed memory said concerning that man who had conceived a passion for her and was inclined toward death on account of the abundance of his desire for her — and the physicians said he had no remedy until she has relations with him or stands naked before him — they asked the Sages of blessed memory and they said: let him die and let her not have relations with him; let him die and let her not stand naked before him; let him die and let her not even converse with him behind the fence — because of the protective fence against forbidden relations and the blemish to family lineage. And just as the betrothed girl was given to be saved by the life of the violator, as it is said, 'and she has no savior' (ואין מושיע לה) — this teaches that if she does have a savior, he is obligated to save her by whatever means he can — so too the murderer was given to be saved by his life, that is, whoever sees a person pursuing his fellow to kill him is obligated to save the pursued person by the life of the pursuer." "<b>And for this reason</b> our Rabbis of blessed memory permitted, in the case of a woman in difficult labor who is in great danger, that it is permitted to kill the fetus in order to save the mother. This is by the law of the pursuer (רודף), for this fetus is oppressing its mother and seeks to kill her, and since this is so it is a pursuer and it is permitted to kill it, and one saves the pursued person — who is the mother — by the life of the pursuer, who is the fetus. Therefore it is permitted to give her a potion or to fumigate her so that the child dies and comes out. And when is this? When the fetus has not brought out its head and the majority of it; but if it has brought out its head and the majority, it is as though it were already born, and it is forbidden to touch it, because one does not push aside one life for the sake of another life. And so we learned in Ohalot: 'A woman in difficult labor — one cuts up the fetus in her womb and brings it out limb by limb, because her life takes precedence over its life. If the majority of it has come out, one does not touch it, for one does not push aside one life for the sake of another life.'" "<b>And in Sanhedrin</b>, chapter 'The Rebellious Son,' they raise the question: why does one not push aside? But did you not say that a minor pursuer may be saved by his life — and this one is a pursuer? And they answer: this minor is not a pursuer, for the minor seeks to come out, but it is from Heaven that they are pursuing her. And the sense of 'she cried out' (צעקה) — that is, where it is possible, she cried out. Or one may say: if she cried out and there is no savior for her — for even if it was heard that she did not cry out, she is exempt, since there is no savior for her."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:50:37.805275531Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}